  fld::system::init(options)
  fld::system::add_material(properties)
  fld::system::emit(source)
  fld::system::splash(source)
  fld::system::update(seconds, collision, contact)
  fld::surface::init(grid, width, height, metres_per_uv)
  fld::surface::set_frame(grid, normal, transform)
  fld::surface::set_chart(grid, vertices, triangle_indices)
  fld::surface::deposit(grid, uv, radius_metres, volume_m3)
  fld::surface::update(grid, seconds, fluid_material, porous_material, gravity)
  fld::surface::volume(grid)
  fld::surface::take(grid, x, y, amount_m3)
  fld::porous::absorb(mobile_m3, stored_m3, area_m2, dt, material)
  fld::system::shutdown()

The 3D SPH solver and the finite-volume surface solver share the same fluid
material and density/rheology parameters. Surface transport uses tangent
gravity, a regularized capillary pressure gradient and bounded conservative
face fluxes. Porous uptake transfers liquid from mobile film to pores without
losing mass; wicking uses separate U/V diffusivities for fabric directionality.
A successful film->particle emission explicitly removes the corresponding
volume from its film. Unsupported/failing emission preserves its source mass.

The CPU solver uses physical SI units. It is a real-time thin-film approximation,
not a complete 3D Navier-Stokes/free-surface solve on every cloth fibre and
not a validated vascular injury or forensic reconstruction.
